{"name":"Copper Pyrite Green","hex":"#3e4939","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"copper","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A reddish-brown metallic chemical element (symbol Cu) with the atomic number 29; also, the metal made up of this element.","The reddish-brown colour of copper (etymology 1, noun sense 1)."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of copper (etymology 1, noun sense 1).","Having the reddish-brown colour of copper."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/copper"},{"term":"pyrite","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The common mineral iron disulfide (FeS2), of a pale brass-yellow color and brilliant metallic luster, crystallizing in the isometric system.","(usually as a plural: pyrites) Any metallic-looking sulphide, such as the above, which is the most common."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pyrite"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Chalcopyrite","blocks":[{"text":"Chalcopyrite ( KAL-kə-PY-ryte, -⁠koh-) is a sulfide mineral composed of\ncopper and iron, and the most abundant copper ore mineral. It has the chemical formula CuFeS2 and crystallizes in the tetragonal system. It has a brassy to golden yellow color and a hardness of 3.5 to 4 on the Mohs scale. Its streak is diagnostic as green-tinged black."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Chalcopyrite-199453.jpg/330px-Chalcopyrite-199453.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chalcopyrite","wikidata":"Q111044"},{"title":"Copper","blocks":[{"text":"Copper is a chemical element; it has symbol Cu and atomic number 29. It is a soft, malleable, and ductile metal with very high thermal and electrical conductivity. A freshly exposed surface of pure copper has a pinkish-orange color. Copper is used as a conductor of heat and electricity, as a building material, and as a constituent of various metal alloys, such as sterling silver used in jewelry, cupronickel used to make marine hardware and coins, and constantan used in strain gauges and thermocouples for temperature measurement."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/NatCopper.jpg/330px-NatCopper.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Copper","wikidata":"Q753"},{"title":"Pyrite","blocks":[{"text":"The mineral pyrite ( PY-ryte), or iron pyrite, also known as fool's gold, is an iron sulfide with the chemical formula FeS2 (iron (II) disulfide). Pyrite is the most abundant sulfide mineral."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Pyrite_-_Huanzala_mine%2C_Huallanca%2C_Bolognesi%2C_Ancash%2C_Peru.jpg/330px-Pyrite_-_Huanzala_mine%2C_Huallanca%2C_Bolognesi%2C_Ancash%2C_Peru.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pyrite","wikidata":"Q50769"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Chalcopyrite-unit-cell-3D-balls.png/500px-Chalcopyrite-unit-cell-3D-balls.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Chalcopyrite-unit-cell-3D-balls.png/500px-Chalcopyrite-unit-cell-3D-balls.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The unit cell of chalcopyrite.  Copper is shown in pink, iron in blue and sulfur in yellow.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChalcopyrite-unit-cell-3D-balls.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Chalcopyrite_under_polarized_light.jpg/500px-Chalcopyrite_under_polarized_light.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Chalcopyrite_under_polarized_light.jpg/500px-Chalcopyrite_under_polarized_light.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Microscopic picture of chalcopyrite","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChalcopyrite_under_polarized_light.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Pyrite-Chalcopyrite-Sphalerite-40297.jpg/500px-Pyrite-Chalcopyrite-Sphalerite-40297.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail